The Fitness for Purpose of Analytical Methods — Eurachem (@met-validation: 0, is #book, #part-of @met-sources) { r_5XXJY86AVZZ7XRT71EY7TRYDJY
H. Cantwell, editor. The Fitness for Purpose of Analytical Methods: A Laboratory Guide to Method Validation and Related Topics, third edition, 2025. Eurachem. [Official guide and supplements](https://www.eurachem.org/index.php/publications/guides/mv).

Read the full guide, including its statistical annexes, and the validation-planning supplement where the study requires it. Use the 2025 edition for the current reading rather than silently assigning the older 2014 edition. Compare sampling and handling, precision, bias/trueness, selectivity, working range, detection and quantification, robustness, reporting, and continuing quality control.

The examples concern analytical methods, but the distinction between validation for an intended use and verification of a stated performance is useful across measurement families. A specialized method still needs its own physical and chemical knowledge.

Eurachem offers authorized no-cost access and retains its publication and translation/reproduction terms. Current edition, editor, contents, and availability checked on 28 October 2026. This Record references the guide; it does not adapt or store it.

**Return:** [Measurement and metrology sources — books, guides, and research](../Sources.lingua#r_6H584EJ2Z9D11PG89Y21Y8T1A3).
